---
description: Keep RockServer source modular and avoid growing god files
alwaysApply: true
---

# RockServer module boundaries

- Keep every source file focused on one cohesive responsibility. Do not grow a mixed
  transport, protocol, domain, persistence, lifecycle, and test implementation into one
  large file.
- When a change adds an independent responsibility, extract it immediately into a clearly
  named child module. Preserve existing public module paths through thin facades and
  compatibility re-exports where callers rely on them.
- Separate protocol DTOs, HTTP/WebSocket transport, domain models and validation,
  persistence, lifecycle/orchestration, provider adapters, and tests when they are
  independently readable and testable. Colocate tests with their domain in a `tests.rs`
  child module when a production file becomes hard to scan.
- Prefer small structs with explicit ownership. Introduce a trait only at a real boundary
  with multiple implementations or a necessary fake; do not create a trait, factory, or
  generic abstraction for one implementation.
- Before adding substantial code to an already large file, inspect callers and nearby
  modules, then choose the smallest coherent extraction. Preserve public API, OpenAPI,
  serialization, error, auth, and persistence behavior during structural refactors.
- Add a short `//!` comment to a nontrivial module: responsibility, key invariant, and
  intentional exclusions. Do not duplicate implementation in comments.
- Keep `src/ARCHITECTURE.md` accurate when module boundaries or reading order materially
  change.
